<article class="env-lemma" id="016B">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/016B">Lemma <span data-tag="016B">14.3.2</span>.</a> Let $\mathcal{C}$ be a category. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
Given a simplicial object $U$ in $\mathcal{C}$ we obtain a sequence of objects $U_ n = U([n])$ endowed with the morphisms $d^ n_ j = U(\delta ^ n_ j) : U_ n \to U_{n-1}$ and $s^ n_ j = U(\sigma ^ n_ j) : U_ n \to U_{n + 1}$. These morphisms satisfy the opposites of the relations displayed in Lemma <a href="/tag/0167" data-tag="0167">14.2.3</a>, namely 
</p>
<ol>
  <li><p>
If $0 \leq i < j \leq n + 1$, then $d^ n_ i \circ d^{n + 1}_ j = d^ n_{j - 1} \circ d^{n + 1}_ i$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $0 \leq i < j \leq n - 1$, then $d^ n_ i \circ s^{n - 1}_ j = s^{n - 2}_{j - 1} \circ d^{n - 1}_ i$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $0 \leq j \leq n - 1$, then $\text{id} = d^ n_ j \circ s^{n - 1}_ j = d^ n_{j + 1} \circ s^{n - 1}_ j$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $0 < j + 1 < i \leq n$, then $d^ n_ i \circ s^{n - 1}_ j = s^{n - 2}_ j \circ d^{n - 1}_{i - 1}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If $0 \leq i \leq j \leq n - 1$, then $s^ n_ i \circ s^{n - 1}_ j = s^ n_{j + 1} \circ s^{n - 1}_ i$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</li><li><p>
Conversely, given a sequence of objects $U_ n$ and morphisms $d^ n_ j$, $s^ n_ j$ satisfying (1)(a) – (e) there exists a unique simplicial object $U$ in $\mathcal{C}$ such that $U_ n = U([n])$, $d^ n_ j = U(\delta ^ n_ j)$, and $s^ n_ j = U(\sigma ^ n_ j)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
A morphism between simplicial objects $U$ and $U'$ is given by a family of morphisms $U_ n \to U'_ n$ commuting with the morphisms $d^ n_ j$ and $s^ n_ j$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0168" data-tag="0168">14.2.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>